Let $V = \mathbb{C}^4 \otimes \mathbb{C}^4 \otimes \mathbb{C}^4$, identified with the space of $4\times 4\times 4$ complex arrays $(p_{ijk})_{i,j,k\in\{1,2,3,4\}}$. Define the variety $X \subseteq V$ to be the Zariski closure of the set of tensors of rank at most $4$, i.e. those admitting a decomposition
$$p_{ijk} = \sum_{r=1}^{4} a^{(r)}_i\, b^{(r)}_j\, c^{(r)}_k$$
for some vectors $a^{(r)}, b^{(r)}, c^{(r)} \in \mathbb{C}^4$. Consider the following families of polynomials, each known to vanish identically on $X$:
(i) the degree-$5$ Strassen commutativity polynomials, obtained from the condition that, for any choice of two parallel slices of the tensor, suitable $3\times 3$ minor relations vanish;
(ii) the degree-$6$ Landsberg–Manivel polynomials arising from $\mathrm{GL}(4)^{\times 3}$-representation theory;
(iii) the degree-$9$ symmetrization polynomials.
Let $I_9 \subseteq \mathbb{C}[p_{ijk}]$ be the ideal generated by all such polynomials of degree at most $9$. Question: does the zero locus $V(I_9) \subseteq V$ equal $X$ set-theoretically?

