Let $d_1, d_2$ be positive integers and let $A$ be a positive semidefinite matrix acting on a $d_1 d_2$-dimensional complex Hilbert space, written as a $d_2 \times d_2$ array of blocks $A_{jk}$, $1 \le j,k \le d_2$, each of size $d_1 \times d_1$. Let $M = \sum_{j=1}^{d_2} A_{jj}$ denote the sum of its diagonal blocks (which is a $d_1 \times d_1$ positive semidefinite matrix).

Determine whether one can always find $d_2$ positive semidefinite block matrices $B_1, B_2, \dots, B_{d_2}$ of the same block structure (each is $d_1 d_2 \times d_1 d_2$, partitioned into $d_2 \times d_2$ blocks of size $d_1 \times d_1$), such that:
1. $\operatorname{rank}(B_m) \le d_1$ for every $m = 1, \dots, d_2$;
2. $\sum_{j=1}^{d_2} (B_m)_{jj} = M$ for every $m = 1, \dots, d_2$;
3. $A = \dfrac{1}{d_2} \sum_{m=1}^{d_2} B_m$.

