Background. The Deift–Zhou nonlinear steepest-descent method provides asymptotic analysis of oscillatory matrix Riemann–Hilbert problems: given an oriented contour Σ ⊂ ℂ and a jump matrix V(z; t) depending on a large parameter, one seeks a sectionally analytic function Y(z) with Y(z)→I as z→∞ satisfying Y_+(z) = Y_-(z) V(z; t) on Σ, and one extracts large-parameter asymptotics by deforming contours and constructing local and global parametrices. For 2×2 problems (e.g., orthogonal polynomials, one-matrix models, KdV) and for certain 3×3 problems (e.g., associated with two specific tractable settings), this analysis has been carried out in detail.

Problem. Develop a general nonlinear steepest-descent / Riemann–Hilbert asymptotic method that applies to generic n×n matrix Riemann–Hilbert problems for arbitrary n ≥ 3, in a form sufficient to analyze:

(a) the biorthogonal / multi-orthogonal polynomial Riemann–Hilbert problems that arise in multi-matrix random matrix models (e.g., chains of coupled Hermitian matrices with potentials V_1,…,V_p and coupling constants), and Hermitian matrix models with an external source A (probability measure proportional to exp(−n tr(V(M) − AM)) dM);

(b) systems of multiple orthogonal polynomials with respect to several weights w_1,…,w_r on ℝ (both type I and type II), including the determination of strong large-degree asymptotics of these polynomials and of the associated correlation kernels;

(c) Hermite–Padé (simultaneous Padé) approximation problems for tuples of analytic functions, with uniform error estimates;

(d) consequent arithmetic applications, such as irrationality and linear-independence-over-ℚ statements for values of classical constants and special functions.

Specifically: produce, for arbitrary n, (i) a g-function / equilibrium-measure framework on a multi-sheeted Riemann surface that yields the appropriate global parametrix, (ii) a construction of local parametrices at branch points and other critical points generalizing the Airy and Painlevé parametrices known in the 2×2 and 3×3 cases, and (iii) a small-norm Riemann–Hilbert argument that establishes the resulting asymptotic expansion uniformly in the relevant parameters.

