Let $N \geq 2$ and $1 \leq W \leq N-1$ be integers. A symmetric random band matrix of dimension $N$ with bandwidth $W$ is an $N\times N$ real symmetric random matrix $M = (M_{ij})$ such that the entries $\{M_{ij} : i \le j\}$ are independent, $M_{ij} = 0$ whenever $|i-j| > W$, and the nonzero entries are centered with a common variance scaling (e.g.\ $\mathbb{E}|M_{ij}|^2 = 1/W$ for $|i-j|\le W$).

It is expected that, after appropriate rescaling, the bulk local eigenvalue statistics of $M$ exhibit a sharp transition as $W$ varies with $N\to\infty$:
\begin{itemize}
\item if $W$ is small, the local eigenvalue statistics in the bulk converge to a Poisson point process;
\item if $W$ is large, the local eigenvalue statistics in the bulk converge to the Gaudin–Mehta (GOE sine-kernel) process.
\end{itemize}

Problem. Prove that there is a sharp transition in the bulk local eigenvalue statistics of symmetric random band matrices, and determine the threshold. Specifically, show that the transition occurs in a narrow window around $W \asymp \sqrt{N}$: namely, the bulk local statistics are Poisson when $W \ll \sqrt{N}$ and Gaudin–Mehta when $W \gg \sqrt{N}$, as $N \to \infty$.

