Let $(X,g)$ be a compact Riemannian manifold (possibly with boundary) whose geodesic flow is ergodic / chaotic, and let $\{\psi_k\}_{k\ge 1}$ be an orthonormal basis of real-valued eigenfunctions of the (Dirichlet) Laplace–Beltrami operator with eigenvalues $0\le \lambda_1^2\le \lambda_2^2 \le \cdots \to \infty$, so that $-\Delta\psi_k=\lambda_k^2\psi_k$. For each $k$ and a base point $x_0\in X$, consider the rescaled eigenfunction at the wavelength scale,
$$\Psi_{k,x_0}(y) := \psi_k\!\left(\exp_{x_0}(y/\lambda_k)\right), \qquad y\in T_{x_0}X,$$
viewed on balls of radius $R$ (microscopic scale, $R$ fixed) or radius $R_k\to\infty$ with $R_k=o(\lambda_k)$ (intermediate / mesoscopic scale).

Let $\Phi$ denote the isotropic monochromatic Gaussian random wave on $\mathbb{R}^d$ ($d=\dim X$): the centered Gaussian field with covariance equal to the spherical average of $e^{i\langle\xi,\,\cdot\,\rangle}$ over unit vectors $\xi\in S^{d-1}$ (Berry's random wave model). For manifolds with boundary, replace $\Phi$ by the appropriate boundary-adapted Gaussian ensemble.

Question. Prove or disprove: for a "generic" chaotic $(X,g)$, along a density-one subsequence of eigenfunctions, and for almost every base point $x_0\in X$, the rescaled fields $\Psi_{k,x_0}$ converge in distribution (in $C^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}$, or as random tempered distributions) to $\Phi$, both at microscopic scale and at every intermediate scale $1\ll R_k\ll \lambda_k$. Equivalently, establish rigorously that the statistics of high-energy chaotic eigenfunctions agree with those of the Gaussian random-wave ensemble for the following observables:
(i) the pointwise value distribution of $\psi_k$;
(ii) two-point and higher correlation functions at separations of order $\lambda_k^{-1}$;
(iii) the geometry and topology of the nodal set $\{x:\psi_k(x)=0\}$ (e.g. Hausdorff measure, Euler characteristic);
(iv) the number, volumes, and topology of nodal domains (connected components of $\{\psi_k\ne 0\}$).

