Background. In Bayesian inference, given a hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}$ one assigns a prior probability distribution over $\mathcal{H}$. A guiding "symmetry principle" says that, absent further information, one should treat hypotheses symmetrically. For a finite class $\mathcal{H} = \{h_1,\dots,h_n\}$ this is realized by the uniform prior $P(h_i)=1/n$. When $\mathcal{H}$ is parameterized by $\theta$ in a smooth manifold $\Theta$, a natural strengthening is reparametrization invariance: if $\phi=g(\theta)$ is a smooth bijective change of coordinates, then the prior densities $p_\Theta$ and $p_\Phi$ should be related by the usual Jacobian rule, so that the induced measure on $\mathcal{H}$ is independent of the chosen parameterization. For a regular parametric family with Fisher information matrix $I(\theta)$, the Jeffreys prior $p(\theta)\propto \sqrt{\det I(\theta)}$ is reparametrization invariant; on a compact parameter space it can typically be normalized to a proper probability distribution. On non-compact parameter spaces such as $\Theta=\mathbb{N}$ (or any countably infinite discrete set) or $\Theta=\mathbb{R}$ (or $\mathbb{R}^d$), the Jeffreys prior and other symmetry-derived rules frequently yield improper (non-normalizable) distributions, which are often unsuitable as genuine priors (for example, they need not give well-defined predictive distributions or admit a coherent decision-theoretic interpretation).

Problem. Formulate and justify a general principle for assigning a prior probability distribution to an arbitrary (possibly non-compact, infinite, discrete or continuous) hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}$ such that:
(1) it reduces to the uniform prior when $\mathcal{H}$ is finite;
(2) it is invariant under a suitably specified group of admissible reparametrizations / symmetries of $\mathcal{H}$;
(3) it always yields a proper (normalizable) probability distribution on $\mathcal{H}$, in particular on $\mathcal{H}$ parameterized by $\mathbb{N}$ or by $\mathbb{R}^d$;
(4) it agrees, in the regular compact case, with Jeffreys' prior (or with another well-motivated invariant prior).

Either exhibit such a construction together with the precise symmetry group it respects and a proof of properness and invariance, or prove an impossibility result showing that no construction can simultaneously satisfy (1)–(4) under a stated minimal set of axioms.

