Donaldson–Thomas (DT) theory on a smooth projective Calabi–Yau threefold $X$ produces deformation-invariant integer-valued counts of coherent sheaves on $X$, defined by integrating against a virtual fundamental class on moduli spaces of stable sheaves (or ideal sheaves) of fixed Chern character, whose existence relies on the symmetric perfect obstruction theory associated with Serre duality in dimension 3.

Provide a mathematically sound framework that extends this construction to smooth projective varieties (in particular, Calabi–Yau manifolds) of complex dimension strictly greater than 3. Specifically, for $\dim_{\mathbb{C}} X > 3$, define enumerative invariants counting (semi)stable coherent sheaves, or ideal sheaves of subschemes, on $X$ that:

(a) are constructed via a virtual fundamental class on appropriate moduli spaces;
(b) are deformation invariant under deformations of the complex structure of $X$;
(c) reduce to, or naturally generalize, the standard Donaldson–Thomas invariants when $\dim_{\mathbb{C}} X = 3$;
(d) admit a sheaf-theoretic / algebro-geometric definition compatible with expected wall-crossing and generating-function structures.

The problem is to give such a definition in dimensions $> 3$ (and, in particular, to determine to what extent and in which dimensions $> 3$ such a theory can exist).

