Setting. Consider the framework of universal algorithmic agents acting in computable environments. Let an environment μ be a computable probability measure over interaction histories of an agent receiving percepts (observations and rewards r_t ∈ [0,1]) and emitting actions, and let π denote a policy (agent). Define the expected discounted future value V^π_μ of policy π in environment μ in the standard universal-AI manner. The (incomputable) intelligence order relation (IOR) declares π ⪰ π' iff V^π_μ ≥ V^{π'}_μ for every computable environment μ in some reference class, weighted (or quantified) by a universal prior such as the Solomonoff prior 2^{−K(μ)}.

Problem. Construct a computable, resource-bounded surrogate ⪰_c of the intelligence order relation (or of a meaningful restricted special case of it) such that:

(a) ⪰_c is decidable, or at least effectively approximable, given explicit time and space resource bounds on both the policies being compared and on the environments in the reference class;

(b) ⪰_c induces a practically implementable intelligence test for agents, in analogy with how the C-Test for compression-based intelligence was obtained from the incomputable Kt-complexity-based definition of universal sequence prediction;

(c) ⪰_c is justified by an explicit choice of resource-bounded complexity measure (for example Levin's Kt complexity, Schmidhuber's Speed Prior, time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity K^t, distinguishing complexity, or another variant from algorithmic information theory).

Determine which notion of resource-bounded complexity, or which alternative universal-style prior, is most appropriate for defining ⪰_c, and prove that the resulting relation (i) refines or approximates the original IOR in a precise sense (e.g. agreeing with it in the limit of unbounded resources), (ii) is computable within stated resource bounds, and (iii) yields a feasible benchmark protocol that can rank concrete agents.

