Consider a closed quantum cosmological system whose global state is a vector $|\Psi(t)\rangle$ in a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_S \otimes \mathcal{H}_E$ (system tensor environment), evolving by a unitary group $U(t) = e^{-iHt/\hbar}$ generated by a time-reversal-symmetric Hamiltonian $H$. Decoherence is the suppression of off-diagonal elements of the reduced density matrix $\rho_S(t) = \mathrm{Tr}_E |\Psi(t)\rangle\langle \Psi(t)|$ in some quasi-classical pointer basis $\{|q\rangle\}$, i.e. $\langle q|\rho_S(t)|q'\rangle \to 0$ for $q\neq q'$ as $t$ increases.

Two candidate accounts of measurement are admitted:
(a) a non-unitary collapse law replacing $U(t)$ at random times by a projection, yielding a time-asymmetric dynamics;
(b) purely unitary Everettian branching, in which the unitarily evolved $|\Psi(t)\rangle$ decomposes into approximately orthogonal, dynamically autonomous components ("branches"/quasi-classical worlds).

The problem is the following. Identify, in mathematically explicit form, a condition $\mathcal{C}$ on the initial global wave function $|\Psi(t_0)\rangle$ (an "initial-state hypothesis") such that, under unitary evolution by $H$:
1. For all $t > t_0$ the reduced state $\rho_S(t)$ exhibits monotone (or near-monotone) decoherence in a preferred quasi-classical basis, while recoherence — i.e. the spontaneous return of off-diagonal coherences — is generically suppressed for cosmologically relevant timescales;
2. The unitary evolution of $|\Psi(t)\rangle$ admits a stable branching decomposition $|\Psi(t)\rangle = \sum_\alpha c_\alpha(t) |\psi_\alpha(t)\rangle$ into quasi-classical worlds carrying a definite forward temporal orientation (records, retarded correlations);
3. In the alternative scenario (a), the same condition $\mathcal{C}$ on $|\Psi(t_0)\rangle$ accounts for the observed time-asymmetry of decoherence (retarded rather than advanced entanglement), in tandem with the time-asymmetric collapse law.

Determine such a condition $\mathcal{C}$, prove that it implies (1)–(3), and decide whether (b) alone (no collapse) suffices, given $\mathcal{C}$, to recover a uniquely oriented arrow of time within the resulting Everettian branch structure.

