Let $F$ be an infinite field and let $n \geq 2$ be an integer. For $m \geq 2$, let $X_1, \ldots, X_m$ be $m$ generic $n \times n$ matrices, i.e., matrices whose entries $\{x_{ij}^{(k)} : 1 \le i,j \le n,\ 1 \le k \le m\}$ are algebraically independent commuting indeterminates over $F$. Let $F\{X_1,\ldots,X_m\}$ denote the $F$-subalgebra of $M_n(F(x_{ij}^{(k)}))$ generated by $X_1,\ldots,X_m$ (the ring of $m$ generic $n\times n$ matrices), and let $UD(F,m,n)$ denote its ring of central quotients (the generic division algebra). Define:
\begin{itemize}
\item $Z_m(F,n)$: the center of $UD(F,m,n)$;
\item $Z(F,n) := Z_2(F,n)$: the center of the generic division algebra on two generic $n\times n$ matrices;
\item $Z_H(F,n)$: the center of the trace ring of generic $n\times n$ matrices (the so-called Procesi/Formanek invariant field for the action of $\mathrm{PGL}_n$).
\end{itemize}
Recall that an extension $K/F$ is called:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{rational} if $K \cong F(t_1, \ldots, t_r)$ is purely transcendental over $F$;
\item \emph{stably rational} if $K(s_1, \ldots, s_\ell)$ is rational over $F$ for some independent indeterminates $s_i$;
\item \emph{retract rational} if there is an $F$-algebra inclusion $K \hookrightarrow F(t_1,\ldots,t_r)_{\mathfrak p}$ (a localization of a polynomial ring) admitting an $F$-algebra retraction onto a domain whose field of fractions is $K$.
\end{itemize}
Determine, for each $n \geq 2$ and each $m \geq 2$, whether the field extensions $Z(F,n)/F$, $Z_m(F,n)/F$, and $Z_H(F,n)/F$ are rational, stably rational, or retract rational.

