Let F be a field and let Br(F) denote its Brauer group, i.e. the group of equivalence classes of finite-dimensional central simple F-algebras under tensor product, where two algebras are equivalent if they are isomorphic to matrix algebras over a common central division F-algebra. For a prime number p, let (Br F)_p denote the p-primary torsion subgroup of Br(F), consisting of all elements whose order is a power of p. A subgroup D of an abelian group A is called divisible if for every d ∈ D and every positive integer n there exists d' ∈ D with nd' = d. An abelian group is called an elementary abelian 2-group if every nonzero element has order 2.

Prove or disprove: for every field F and every prime p, at least one of the following three conditions holds:
(a) (Br F)_p = 0;
(b) (Br F)_p contains a nonzero divisible subgroup;
(c) p = 2 and (Br F)_2 is an elementary abelian 2-group.

