Let F be a field and let Br(F) denote its Brauer group, i.e. the group of equivalence classes of finite-dimensional central simple F-algebras under tensor product, where two algebras are equivalent if they are isomorphic to matrix algebras over a common central division F-algebra. For a prime number p, let (Br F)_p denote the p-primary torsion subgroup of Br(F), consisting of all elements whose order is a power of p. A subgroup D of an abelian group A is called divisible if for every d ∈ D and every positive integer n there exists d' ∈ D with nd' = d. An abelian group is called an elementary abelian 2-group if every nonzero element has order 2.

Prove or disprove: for every field F and every prime p, at least one of the following three conditions holds:
(a) (Br F)_p = 0;
(b) (Br F)_p contains a nonzero divisible subgroup;
(c) p = 2 and (Br F)_2 is an elementary abelian 2-group.

representations.normalized:
Consider low-mass Population II red giants (metal-poor stars with mass ≲ 1 M☉, typified by stars in Galactic globular clusters) evolving along the Red Giant Branch (RGB) and the Asymptotic Giant Branch (AGB). During these phases such stars lose a non-negligible fraction of their envelope through stellar winds, and the integrated mass loss controls subsequent evolution (horizontal-branch morphology, post-AGB tracks, white-dwarf masses, surface chemistry).

In current stellar evolution computations the mass-loss rate Ṁ is typically incorporated through empirical prescriptions (e.g. a Reimers-type law Ṁ = −η L R / M with a tunable efficiency parameter η, or the Bloecker/Vassiliadis–Wood prescriptions for the AGB) that were calibrated against bright Population I giants and then extrapolated to the Population II regime.

Provide a description of mass loss along the RGB and AGB of Population II giants that satisfies all of the following:

(a) it is physically grounded, i.e. derived from an identified driving mechanism (radiation pressure on dust and molecules, acoustic/Alfvén wave or magnetically driven winds, pulsation-driven outflows, chromospheric activity, binarity, or a specified combination thereof) rather than a purely empirical fitting formula;

(b) it specifies the characteristic mass-loss timescale τ_ML = M/Ṁ as a function of stellar parameters along each evolutionary phase;

(c) it gives the explicit functional dependence of the mass-loss efficiency on the global stellar parameters (luminosity L, effective temperature T_eff, radius R, current mass M, surface gravity g) and on the metallicity Z (or [Fe/H]);

(d) it is calibrated against, and consistent with, empirical determinations of total RGB and AGB mass loss obtained directly for Population II systems (for example from horizontal-branch and white-dwarf masses in globular clusters of varying metallicity, and from circumstellar dust emission of metal-poor giants);

(e) it can be implemented in stellar evolution codes without requiring extrapolation of Population I calibrations into the Population II regime.

The problem is to construct and validate such a mass-loss law.

