In the theory of stellar evolution, a Thermally Pulsing Asymptotic Giant Branch (TPAGB) star undergoes recurrent helium-shell flashes followed by a "third dredge-up" (3DU): an episode in which the convective envelope penetrates inward into regions previously processed by hydrogen and helium burning, mixing freshly synthesized elements (notably carbon and s-process elements) into the surface layers. The location and efficiency of this mixing crucially shape the surface chemical composition of TPAGB stars but, in current one-dimensional stellar evolutionary codes, the boundary between the convective envelope and the underlying radiative intershell region must traverse a layer with steep discontinuities in chemical composition and in radiative opacity. Standard local mixing-length-type prescriptions, together with simple convective-boundary criteria (Schwarzschild or Ledoux), do not by themselves determine a unique inward extent for the dredge-up, and as a consequence existing fully evolutionary AGB calculations parametrize the efficiency of the 3DU by means of one or more tunable free parameters (such as an overshooting length, a diffusive-mixing scale, or an imposed dredge-up parameter λ).

Problem. Formulate, within the framework of a one-dimensional spherically symmetric stellar evolution model coupled to a treatment of time-dependent turbulent convection, a closed mathematical prescription for convective mixing across a stable interface separating two fluids with prescribed jumps in mean molecular weight μ and in Rosseland mean opacity κ, such that:

(a) the prescription is derived from, or rigorously consistent with, the underlying hydrodynamic equations governing convective motions in the presence of composition and opacity gradients (e.g., the Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes equations with chemical species transport);

(b) it provides a uniquely determined inward extent and time evolution of the convective–radiative boundary during a thermal pulse cycle, without recourse to an arbitrary free parameter quantifying the efficiency of the third dredge-up;

(c) when implemented in a TPAGB stellar evolution computation, it reproduces the dredge-up depth and the resulting surface abundance evolution as a deterministic function of the stellar mass, metallicity, and pulse number.

Either exhibit such a prescription and prove that conditions (a)–(c) are satisfied, or prove that within the class of one-dimensional time-dependent convection models no such prescription exists.

