Let $\mathcal{H}$ denote a Hardy field, i.e., a field of germs at $+\infty$ of real-valued functions defined on a half-line, closed under differentiation. A function $a\in\mathcal{H}$ is said to have polynomial growth if there exists an integer $k\ge 0$ such that $|a(t)|/t^{k}\to 0$ as $t\to\infty$. Fix $\ell\ge 1$ and let $F=\{a_1,\ldots,a_\ell\}\subset\mathcal{H}$ be a collection of Hardy-field functions of polynomial growth. Let $\operatorname{span}^{*}(F)$ denote the set of nonzero real linear combinations $a=c_1 a_1+\cdots+c_\ell a_\ell$ with $(c_1,\ldots,c_\ell)\in\mathbb{R}^\ell\setminus\{0\}$.

A family of integer sequences $\{b_1(n),\ldots,b_\ell(n)\}$ is called good for $\ell$-convergence of a single transformation if for every invertible measure-preserving system $(X,\mathcal{B},\mu,T)$ and every $f_1,\ldots,f_\ell\in L^\infty(\mu)$, the averages
$$\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N} f_1(T^{b_1(n)}x)\cdots f_\ell(T^{b_\ell(n)}x)$$
converge in $L^2(\mu)$ as $N\to\infty$.

Show that the family $\{(\lfloor a_1(n)\rfloor),\ldots,(\lfloor a_\ell(n)\rfloor)\}$ is good for $\ell$-convergence of a single transformation if and only if every $a\in\operatorname{span}^{*}(F)$ satisfies at least one of the following:
\begin{itemize}
\item $|a(t)-c\,p(t)|/\log t \to \infty$ as $t\to\infty$ for every $c\in\mathbb{R}$ and every $p\in\mathbb{Z}[t]$;
\item $a(t)-c\,p(t)\to d$ as $t\to\infty$ for some $c,d\in\mathbb{R}$ and some $p\in\mathbb{Z}[t]$;
\item $|a(t)-t/m|\le C\log t$ for all sufficiently large $t$, for some nonzero $m\in\mathbb{Z}$ and some $C>0$.
\end{itemize}

