Let $N\geq 1$ and let $a_0,a_1,\dots,a_N$ be real numbers. Consider the homogeneous linear iterative functional equation
$$\sum_{j=0}^{N} a_j\,\varphi^{j}(x)=0\qquad\text{for all }x\in(0,\infty),\tag{$\ast$}$$
where $\varphi:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ is the unknown function, $\varphi^{0}(x)=x$, and $\varphi^{j}=\varphi\circ\varphi^{j-1}$ for $j\geq 1$. Associated with $(\ast)$ is the characteristic polynomial
$$P(\lambda)=\sum_{j=0}^{N}a_j\lambda^{j},$$
and let $\Lambda=\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}:P(\lambda)=0\}$ denote its set of roots. Fix $c\in\Lambda\cap(0,\infty)$, and consider the following three conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $c$ is a simple root of $P$;
\item[(ii)] $(\Lambda\setminus\{c\})\cap(0,\infty)=\emptyset$, i.e., no other positive real number is a root;
\item[(iii)] every $\lambda\in\Lambda\setminus\{c\}$ satisfies $|\lambda|>c$.
\end{itemize}
It is known that conditions (i)–(iii) are sufficient to guarantee that $(\ast)$ admits at most one continuous, monotonic solution $\varphi:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$.

Question: Are conditions (i), (ii), (iii) also necessary for the uniqueness of the continuous monotonic solution $\varphi:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)$ of $(\ast)$? In other words, if uniqueness of such a solution holds, must each of (i), (ii), (iii) be satisfied?

