Background. In group-based (noncommutative) cryptography, a *platform group* is a (typically infinite, finitely presented, non-abelian) group $G$ in which cryptographic primitives—such as Diffie–Hellman-style key exchange (e.g. via commuting subgroups or conjugacy), digital signature schemes, and authentication protocols—are instantiated. The security of such schemes is generally argued by reducing the task of an adversary to the hardness of an algorithmic problem in $G$ (for example, the conjugacy search problem, the simultaneous conjugacy search problem, the decomposition problem, the membership problem, or variants of the word/geodesic length problem), often relative to a chosen probability distribution on keys.

Problem. Develop a general framework, together with verifiable criteria on a finitely presented group $G$ and on a probability distribution $\mathcal D$ on its elements, under which one can give a rigorous reduction-style proof of security (in a precisely defined adversarial model such as IND-CPA, IND-CCA, EUF-CMA, or analogous interactive authentication-soundness models) for noncommutative cryptographic primitives—public-key exchange, digital signatures, and authentication—built over $G$. In particular, identify explicit candidate platform groups $G$ and explicit hardness assumptions $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$ such that:
(i) $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$ can be precisely formulated as a search or decision problem with respect to $\mathcal D$;
(ii) for at least one of the primitives above, one can prove a polynomial-time reduction from breaking the primitive (in the chosen security model) to solving $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$;
(iii) there is supporting evidence (worst-case to average-case reductions, generic-case complexity lower bounds, or unconditional lower bounds in restricted models) that $\mathcal H(G,\mathcal D)$ is intractable.

Equivalently: produce a noncommutative platform group together with associated cryptographic schemes that are *provably secure* in the standard sense of modern provable cryptography.

