Let $L$ be a fixed finite labeled leaf set with $|L|=n\ge 2$. A discrete time-tree on $L$ is a rooted binary phylogenetic tree with leaf set $L$ together with an assignment of pairwise distinct non-negative integer times to its nodes such that (i) every leaf has time $0$ when required by the model, and (ii) the time of each non-root node is strictly smaller than the time of its parent. Given a discrete time-tree $T$ with internal node times $t_1<t_2<\cdots<t_{n-1}<t_n$ (where $t_n$ is the root time), the event intervals of $T$ are the intervals $I_k=[t_k,t_{k+1}]$ for $k=0,\dots,n-1$ (with $t_0=0$); the length of $I_k$ is $t_{k+1}-t_k$.

Define the following local moves:
- (length move on $I_k$) increase or decrease the length of one event interval $I_k$ by $1$, keeping the lengths of all other intervals the same (and all node times non-negative, distinct, and respecting the parent–child time order);
- (rank move on $I_k$) when $I_k$ has length $1$, swap the times (ranks) of the two internal nodes bounding $I_k$, provided neither is the parent of the other;
- (NNI move on $I_k$) when $I_k$ has length $1$ and the upper endpoint of $I_k$ is the parent of the lower endpoint, perform a nearest-neighbor interchange across the edge connecting them.

Consider the four graphs whose vertex sets are discrete time-trees on $L$ and whose edges correspond to single allowed moves:
- $\mathrm{DtT}^m$: ranked discrete time-trees with leaves all at time $0$, all three move types allowed;
- $\mathrm{DtT}^{um}$: ultrametric variant where leaf times may differ, all three move types allowed;
- $\mathrm{RNNI}$: only rank moves and NNI moves are allowed (every event interval has length $1$);
- $\mathrm{RNNI}^u$: ultrametric variant of $\mathrm{RNNI}$.

For $G\in\{\mathrm{DtT}^m,\mathrm{DtT}^{um},\mathrm{RNNI},\mathrm{RNNI}^u\}$ and two trees $T,R$ in $V(G)$, let $d_G(T,R)$ be the minimum number of edges in a path from $T$ to $R$ in $G$.

Determine the computational complexity of computing $d_G(T,R)$ given $T$ and $R$ as input, for each of the four graphs $G$ above.

