Let M be a smooth (finite-dimensional) manifold, U a nonempty set of admissible control values, and F: M × U → TM a set-valued or single-valued map assigning to each (x,u) ∈ M × U a tangent vector F(x,u) ∈ T_x M. The triple Σ = (M, U, F) is called a general control system; its trajectories are absolutely continuous curves x: I → M satisfying ẋ(t) = F(x(t), u(t)) for some measurable control u: I → U. Let A ⊆ M be a nonempty closed target set.

A feedback law is a map k: M → U (possibly discontinuous, possibly set-valued) such that the closed-loop differential inclusion ẋ(t) ∈ F(x(t), k(x(t))) admits suitably defined solutions (e.g. Carathéodory, Filippov, or sample-and-hold solutions). The set A is said to be:
  • invariant under the closed-loop dynamics if every solution starting in A remains in A for all positive times;
  • a local attractor if there exists a neighborhood N of A such that every solution starting in N converges to A as t → ∞;
  • locally asymptotically stable under the closed-loop dynamics if it is both (Lyapunov) stable and a local attractor.

Problem. Formulate verifiable sufficient conditions on Σ = (M, U, F) and A — for instance in terms of controllability hypotheses, the existence of (nonsmooth) control-Lyapunov functions, or structural properties such as homogeneity — that:
  (a) guarantee the existence of a feedback law k: M → U rendering A invariant for the closed loop;
  (b) guarantee the existence of a feedback law k for which A is a local attractor of the closed-loop dynamics;
  (c) guarantee the existence of a feedback law k that locally asymptotically stabilizes A; and
  (d) are constructive, in the sense that the proof of the sufficient condition yields an explicit procedure for designing such a feedback k, rather than a pure existence statement.

In addition, identify canonical subclasses of general control systems Σ = (M, U, F) (for example, classes determined by regularity of F, structure of U, or geometric properties of M and A) within which conditions of types (a)–(d) can be formulated and proved.

