Background. Consider a control system on a smooth manifold M of the form ẋ = f(x,u), where u is a control input, and let A ⊂ M be a compact set that one wishes to render asymptotically stable by means of a (continuous or smooth) feedback law u = k(x). A classical topological obstruction, going back to Milnor and Bhat–Bernstein, asserts that if A is a compact attractor of a continuous flow on M with a basin of attraction that retracts onto A, then A and its basin must have the same Euler characteristic; more generally, retraction-theoretic arguments yield necessary conditions for global or semi-global feedback stabilization expressed in terms of the Euler characteristic χ(A) of the target set. When χ(A) ≠ 0, these methods produce sharp obstructions and, in many situations, matching constructive stabilization results.

Many natural targets, however, have χ(A) = 0. Examples include:
 (a) every closed odd-dimensional manifold (so χ = 0 by Poincaré duality);
 (b) every compact connected Lie group of positive dimension;
 (c) any nontrivial periodic orbit of a flow (a circle).
In such cases retraction-theoretic invariants based on Euler characteristic are silent, and the existing theory does not provide a fine classification of which compact target sets A with χ(A) = 0 can be (locally, semi-globally, or globally) asymptotically stabilized by continuous, smooth, time-varying, or discontinuous feedback, nor does it provide sharp obstructions in this regime.

Problem. Develop a theory of topological and dynamical invariants, going beyond Euler characteristic and pure retraction theory, that:
 1. produces necessary conditions (obstructions) for the existence of feedback stabilizers (continuous, smooth, time-varying, sampled, or discontinuous in a precisely specified sense) of a compact set A ⊂ M with χ(A) = 0 in a control system ẋ = f(x,u);
 2. produces matching sufficient conditions, ideally constructive, in the same regime;
 3. yields a finer classification of compact zero-Euler-characteristic target sets A according to their stabilizability properties, and in particular distinguishes among the classes (a)–(c) above.

Concretely, identify invariants (e.g. coming from K-theory, equivariant cohomology, characteristic classes of normal bundles, Lusternik–Schnirelmann category, vector field indices on bundles, or homotopy-theoretic invariants of pairs (basin, A)) whose vanishing or nonvanishing characterizes the existence of the relevant stabilizing feedback for a given regularity class, and prove the corresponding obstruction/realization theorems.

