Let $M_n$ denote the algebra of $n\times n$ complex matrices. A matrix $A\in M_k$ is said to be a compression of $B\in M_n$ (where $k\le n$) if there exists an isometry $V:\mathbb{C}^k\to\mathbb{C}^n$ (equivalently, an $n\times k$ matrix $V$ with $V^*V=I_k$) such that $A=V^*BV$. A matrix is normal if it commutes with its adjoint.

Given complex numbers $a_1,\dots,a_k$ and $b_1,\dots,b_n$ (with $k\le n$), find necessary and sufficient conditions on these two lists for the existence of normal matrices $A\in M_k$ and $B\in M_n$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item the eigenvalues of $A$, counted with multiplicity, are exactly $a_1,\dots,a_k$;
\item the eigenvalues of $B$, counted with multiplicity, are exactly $b_1,\dots,b_n$;
\item $A$ is a compression of $B$.
\end{itemize}
Equivalently, characterize precisely those pairs of multisets $\{a_i\}\subset\mathbb{C}$ and $\{b_j\}\subset\mathbb{C}$ that arise in this manner.

