Let $\mathbb{H} = \{a_0 + a_1 i + a_2 j + a_3 k : a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3 \in \mathbb{R}\}$ denote the skew field of real quaternions, with multiplication determined by $i^2=j^2=k^2=ijk=-1$. A quaternion-valued differential equation (QDE) is a system of the form
$$\dot{x}(t) = A(t)\,x(t) + f(t,x(t)), \qquad x(t)\in \mathbb{H}^n,$$
where $A(t)$ is an $n\times n$ quaternion matrix-valued function and $f$ is a quaternion-valued nonlinearity. A discontinuous QDE is a QDE in which the right-hand side, the state, or the control input is allowed to be discontinuous in $t$ or $x$; standard families of such systems include:
(i) impulsive QDEs, in which the state satisfies $\dot x(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t,x(t))$ for $t\ne t_k$ together with jump conditions $x(t_k^+)-x(t_k^-)=I_k(x(t_k^-))$ at a prescribed sequence $t_1<t_2<\cdots$;
(ii) switched QDEs, where the active vector field is selected from a finite family $\{F_\sigma\}_{\sigma\in\Sigma}$ by a switching signal $\sigma:[0,\infty)\to\Sigma$;
(iii) QDEs on time scales, where the derivative is replaced by the delta-derivative on a closed subset $\mathbb{T}\subset\mathbb{R}$;
(iv) piecewise QDEs, where $\mathbb{H}^n$ is partitioned into regions on which the dynamics differ;
(v) fuzzy QDEs, where the dynamics are governed by a Takagi–Sugeno-type rule base with quaternion-valued consequents.

Develop a unified theory of discontinuous QDEs covering classes (i)–(v): in particular, formulate the appropriate notions of solution (e.g.\ Filippov, Carathéodory, or impulsive solutions in the quaternion setting), establish existence, uniqueness, and continuous dependence theorems, and determine whether the discontinuous control strategies (impulsive control, switching control, sliding-mode control, fuzzy control, time-scale control, etc.) used to stabilize, synchronize, or otherwise control discontinuous real- or complex-valued ordinary differential equations can be adapted to discontinuous QDEs, taking into account the noncommutativity of $\mathbb{H}$. Specifically, identify which design methods extend directly, which require genuinely quaternionic modifications, and provide constructive controllers together with quaternion-valued Lyapunov-type stability criteria.

