A projective plane is an incidence structure consisting of a set of points and a set of lines, together with an incidence relation between points and lines, satisfying the following axioms:

(i) any two distinct lines are incident with exactly one common point;
(ii) any two distinct points are incident with exactly one common line;
(iii) there exist four points, no three of which are incident with a common line.

If such a plane has finitely many points, then there is a positive integer q (called the order of the plane) such that:
- the total number of points equals the total number of lines and equals q^2 + q + 1;
- every line is incident with exactly q + 1 points;
- every point is incident with exactly q + 1 lines.

For every prime power q, there exists a projective plane of order q (for example, the Desarguesian plane PG(2, F_q) over the finite field F_q).

Problem. Classify, up to isomorphism, all finite projective planes. As a (presumably easier) sub-problem, determine the exact set of positive integers q which occur as the order of some finite projective plane. In particular, decide whether every order q must be a prime power.

