Consider a self-bound finite quantum many-fermion system (an atomic nucleus) described by a microscopic Hamiltonian acting on antisymmetric states of A nucleons. Within the time-dependent Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov (TDHFB) framework, large-amplitude collective motion (LACM) is formulated by seeking a low-dimensional collective submanifold M, embedded in the full TDHFB phase space of generalized Slater determinants, that is approximately decoupled from non-collective degrees of freedom and parametrized by a small set of collective coordinates q and conjugate momenta p. For even-even nuclei this program has been carried out non-perturbatively via the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate (ASCC) method and related approaches, in which M is determined self-consistently from moving-frame HFB and moving-frame RPA equations without resorting to a small-amplitude expansion around a single equilibrium.

Problem. Develop a non-perturbative microscopic framework that determines such a collective submanifold, and the associated collective Hamiltonian and quantization, for systems containing one or more unpaired quasiparticles — in particular for odd-A nuclei, and more generally for HFB configurations with several blocked quasiparticles — in such a way that:

(i) the framework treats single-particle (quasiparticle) degrees of freedom and collective degrees of freedom on the same footing, allowing strong, finite-amplitude coupling between them rather than perturbative particle–vibration coupling around a fixed mean field;

(ii) it reduces, in the small-amplitude limit and in the limit of vanishing unpaired quasiparticles, to the established quasiparticle-RPA and ASCC descriptions, respectively;

(iii) it yields well-defined moving-frame equations whose solutions specify the collective coordinates, the inertial functions, and the residual coupling to non-collective quasiparticle excitations along the entire collective path, including regions of shape transition, shape coexistence, and other phenomena involving large amplitudes.

Provide such a framework, or prove that a framework satisfying (i)–(iii) exists and is unique under stated self-consistency requirements.

