Consider a rotating, self-bound many-fermion system (a model for an atomic nucleus) described by a microscopic Hamiltonian H acting on a many-body Hilbert space, together with a time-dependent mean-field framework in which collective dynamics is parametrized by a few collective coordinates q = (q_1,...,q_n) and conjugate momenta p, derived through the adiabatic self-consistent collective coordinate (ASCC) method or equivalent constrained Hartree–Fock–Bogoliubov plus generator-coordinate scheme. Assume the collective potential energy surface V(q; I) (with I the total angular-momentum quantum number generated by cranking about a chosen axis) admits, for sufficiently large I:

(i) a pair of degenerate minima at q = +q_0 and q = -q_0 related by a parity (reflection) operation P, separated by a barrier (these correspond to "left-" and "right-handed" reflection-asymmetric configurations);
(ii) parameter regions in which the barrier between ±q_0 disappears, producing a single minimum at finite octupole deformation (a "transitional" regime toward static reflection-asymmetric shape);
(iii) shallow regions in q-space around configurations of banana (Y_{31}-type triaxial-octupole) or tetrahedral (Y_{32}-type) symmetry.

Problem. Develop, within the microscopic large-amplitude collective motion theory, a quantitative description of the following three phenomena and establish how each depends on the rotational frequency ω (or equivalently on I):

(a) Tunneling splitting ΔE(I) between the symmetric and antisymmetric combinations of the |+q_0⟩ and |−q_0⟩ states as a function of I, including the cranking-induced modification of the inertia tensor M(q;I), the requantized collective Hamiltonian, and Coriolis/time-odd mean-field contributions;
(b) The collective wavefunction and excitation spectrum in the transitional regime where rotation drives the system from a double-well to a single-well octupole-deformed potential, including the I-dependence of the critical control parameter at which the transition occurs;
(c) The large-amplitude zero-point shape-fluctuation dynamics on shallow potential surfaces of banana and tetrahedral type, including computation of the corresponding collective masses, vibrational excitation energies, and electromagnetic transition strengths as functions of I.

Provide a self-consistent derivation, valid beyond small-amplitude RPA, that yields these quantities directly from the underlying microscopic Hamiltonian H.

