Let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$ and $\mathrm{Inv}(S_n)=\{\pi\in S_n : \pi^2=\mathrm{id}\}$ the set of involutions. For $\pi\in S_n$, write:
- $\mathrm{tr}(\pi)$ for the number of fixed points of $\pi$ (the trace of its permutation matrix);
- $\kappa(\pi)$ for the total number of cycles of $\pi$ (including fixed points);
- $c_j(\pi)$ for the number of cycles of $\pi$ of length exactly $j$, so that $\sum_j j\,c_j(\pi)=n$;
- $E(\pi)$ for the number of even-length cycles of $\pi$, so that $E(\pi)=0$ means every cycle of $\pi$ has odd length.

Let $S(k,j)$ be the Stirling numbers of the second kind, and let $B_m(z)$ denote the Bell polynomials defined by $\sum_{m\ge 0} B_m(z)\frac{x^m}{m!}=\exp\!\bigl(z(e^x-1)\bigr)$.

Prove or disprove the following two identities, valid for every positive integer $n$ (and every positive integer $k$ in (i), and every $z$ in (ii)):

(i) $\displaystyle \sum_{\pi\in \mathrm{Inv}(S_n)} \frac{\mathrm{tr}(\pi)^k}{n!} \;=\; \sum_{j=0}^{k} S(k,j)\,\frac{\#\mathrm{Inv}(S_{n-j})}{(n-j)!},$

(ii) $\displaystyle \sum_{\substack{\pi\in S_n\\ E(\pi)=0}} 2^{\kappa(\pi)}\, B_{\kappa(\pi)}(z) \;=\; \sum_{\pi\in S_n} (2z)^{\kappa(\pi)} \prod_{j=1}^{n} j^{\,c_j(\pi)}.$

