Develop a computationally tractable microscopic framework that performs full three-dimensional angular-momentum projection on top of triaxially deformed relativistic mean-field (or analogous nuclear energy-density-functional) reference states, including configuration mixing through the generator coordinate method and the explicit treatment of one- and multi-quasiparticle excitations, and apply it to odd-A and odd-odd chiral-doublet candidate nuclei (such as those in the A ≈ 100, 130, and 190 mass regions). Specifically, design and demonstrate algorithms that:

1. Generate a sufficient set of triaxially deformed quasiparticle vacua and blocked one- and two-quasiparticle configurations spanning the relevant valence space for an odd-mass or odd-odd chiral candidate;
2. Perform three-dimensional angular-momentum projection P^J_{MK} on these quasiparticle states, yielding projected states of good total angular momentum J;
3. Mix the projected configurations (configuration mixing / GCM with triaxial deformation parameters β, γ as generator coordinates) to obtain energy spectra, electromagnetic transition matrix elements (B(M1), B(E2)), and the predicted near-degenerate chiral partner bands;
4. Control the combinatorial growth of the configuration space and the cost of the Pfaffian/overlap evaluations sufficiently to make the full calculation feasible on currently available computational resources, and benchmark the predictions against experimental chiral-doublet candidates rather than only against light even-even test cases such as ²⁴Mg.

Show whether such a fully self-consistent, beyond-mean-field calculation can reproduce the observed level schemes and transition strengths of established chiral-doublet candidates.

