Consider a two-dimensional electron system (e.g., electrons confined to an interface or quantum well in a semiconductor heterostructure) at low temperature, characterized by carrier density n, effective mass m*, Landé g factor g*, and Pauli spin susceptibility χ ∝ g* m*. As n is decreased toward a critical density n_c at which a metal-insulator transition occurs, experiments find that:

(i) m*/m_b grows strongly (by factors of order three or more) and appears to diverge as n → n_c, where m_b is the band mass;
(ii) χ likewise grows strongly and appears to diverge;
(iii) g* remains nearly unchanged, close to its bulk value, so the enhancement of χ is carried almost entirely by m*;
(iv) the enhancement is observed not only in the diffusive transport regime Tτ/ℏ ≪ 1 (where τ is the elastic scattering time) but also deep in the ballistic regime Tτ/ℏ ≫ 1;
(v) the effect appears universal across silicon-based 2D systems with widely different disorder potentials.

Provide a controlled microscopic many-body theory of the interacting 2D electron liquid that:
(a) predicts a strong enhancement, and possible divergence, of m* and χ as n → n_c from the metallic side;
(b) yields a g factor that remains essentially uncorrected;
(c) is valid in the ballistic regime Tτ/ℏ ≫ 1, not only in the diffusive regime;
(d) is independent of the specific disorder realization, in agreement with the observed universality.

In particular, decide whether the divergence of m* arises from a flattening of the single-particle spectrum at the Fermi energy (a fermion-condensation / Khodel–Shaginyan type scenario) or from another mechanism, and derive the resulting density dependence of m*(n) and χ(n) near n_c.

