Consider a coherent polycrystalline solid modeled in a phase-field framework, in which the local crystallographic state is described by a (scalar or quaternion-valued) orientation field θ(x,t), coupled to a structural order parameter φ(x,t) that distinguishes solid and liquid (or that resolves the underlying periodic atomic density, as in phase-field-crystal models). In the coherent regime, neighboring crystalline regions remain connected through their lattice, so that local rotations of θ are not independent degrees of freedom but are kinematically tied to the dislocation density tensor α = ∇ × β^p, where β^p is the plastic distortion: changes of orientation across a region are accommodated by the nucleation, motion, and rearrangement of dislocations rather than by a free local relaxation.

Standard phase-field models of polycrystals postulate a purely relaxational law of the form ∂_t θ = −M δF/δθ for some free-energy functional F[φ,θ] and mobility M, which does not respect this kinematic constraint.

Problem. Derive a tractable, phase-field-compatible evolution equation for the orientation field θ(x,t) in coherent crystalline matter that:
(i) is obtained from, and consistent with, an evolution equation for the dislocation density tensor α (so that ∂_t θ is determined by dislocation transport and reactions, not by independent local relaxation);
(ii) incorporates the long-range elastic interactions between dislocations through the elastic stress field σ generated by α and an applied external strain, with dislocation velocity driven by the Peach–Koehler force;
(iii) accounts for interactions with other defects (grain boundaries, additional phases, free surfaces) represented in the phase-field setting;
(iv) reduces, in suitable limits, to known continuum descriptions of grain-boundary motion and to standard relaxational orientation dynamics where appropriate;
(v) is closed and computationally tractable as a coupled PDE system for (φ, θ, α) (or an equivalent set of fields).

Formulate such an equation and establish its thermodynamic consistency and well-posedness as a model for orientation dynamics in coherent polycrystals.

