Let X be a scheme of finite type over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic zero, let Γ be a finite group acting on X by scheme automorphisms, and let g be an algebra over k equipped with a Γ-action by algebra automorphisms. The equivariant map algebra M(X, g)^Γ is defined as the algebra of Γ-equivariant regular maps X → g, i.e.

   M(X, g)^Γ = { f ∈ g ⊗_k O(X) : (γ · f)(x) = γ · f(γ^{-1} · x) for all γ ∈ Γ, x ∈ X },

with pointwise algebra operations inherited from g.

The representation theory (in particular, the classification of irreducible finite-dimensional representations) of M(X, g)^Γ has been developed in the case where g is a finite-dimensional Lie algebra or finite-dimensional associative algebra. Develop the corresponding representation theory and, in particular, classify the irreducible finite-dimensional representations of M(X, g)^Γ in each of the following cases:

(a) g is a (symmetrizable) Kac–Moody Lie algebra;
(b) g is a Heisenberg Lie algebra;
(c) g is a finite-dimensional alternative algebra;
(d) g is a finite-dimensional Jordan algebra;
(e) more generally, g is an algebra of any other type for which a well-defined notion of finite-dimensional representation exists.

