Consider the leptonic decay $B^+ \to \tau^+ \nu_\tau$ measured at a future high-luminosity $e^+e^-$ $B$-factory operating at the $\Upsilon(4S)$ resonance, in which one $B$ meson of the $B\bar B$ pair is fully reconstructed in a hadronic or semileptonic mode (the "$B_{\rm tag}$") and the signal $B^+ \to \tau^+ \nu_\tau$ decay (the "$B_{\rm sig}$") is reconstructed on the recoil. Let $\mathcal{B} \equiv \mathrm{BR}(B^+ \to \tau^+ \nu_\tau)$ and assume:

(i) the irreducible relative systematic uncertainty on $\mathcal{B}$, dominated by the $B_{\rm tag}$ and $B_{\rm sig}$ reconstruction efficiencies and by $B\bar B$ counting, currently amounts to $\sigma_{\rm sys}/\mathcal{B} \approx 8.7\%$;
(ii) the relative statistical uncertainty scales as $\sigma_{\rm stat}/\mathcal{B} \propto 1/\sqrt{L}$ where $L$ is the integrated luminosity, with $\sigma_{\rm stat}/\mathcal{B} \approx 2 \cdot 8.7\% = 17.4\%$ at the present reference luminosity;
(iii) the total relative uncertainty is $\sigma_{\rm tot}/\mathcal{B} = \sqrt{(\sigma_{\rm stat}/\mathcal{B})^2 + (\sigma_{\rm sys}/\mathcal{B})^2}$.

Question: Is it achievable, by improvements in the analysis techniques used to determine the $B_{\rm tag}$ and $B_{\rm sig}$ reconstruction efficiencies and the $B\bar B$ counting, to reduce the irreducible systematic uncertainty $\sigma_{\rm sys}/\mathcal{B}$ by a factor of two (to roughly $4.35\%$) so that, at an integrated luminosity of $L = 75\,\mathrm{ab}^{-1}$, the measurement is not dominated by systematics and the total uncertainty $\sigma_{\rm tot}/\mathcal{B}$ reaches approximately $4.5\%$?

