Let $V$ be a smooth complex projective variety of complex dimension $n$. For $0 \le p,q \le n$, let $h^{p,q}(V) = \dim_{\mathbb{C}} H^q(V, \Omega_V^p)$ denote the Hodge numbers of $V$, and let the Chern characteristic numbers of $V$ be the integers obtained by evaluating monomials of total degree $n$ in the Chern classes $c_1(TV),\dots,c_n(TV)$ on the fundamental class $[V]$.

Consider rational linear combinations
$$L = \sum_{p,q} a_{p,q}\, h^{p,q} + \sum_{|I|=n} b_I\, c_I,$$
with $a_{p,q}, b_I \in \mathbb{Q}$, viewed as rational-valued functions on the class of smooth complex projective varieties of dimension $n$.

Determine, in each of the following four senses of equivalence, exactly which such linear combinations $L$ are invariants of the equivalence class of $V$:
(i) orientation-preserving diffeomorphism;
(ii) (unoriented) diffeomorphism;
(iii) orientation-preserving homeomorphism;
(iv) (unoriented) homeomorphism.

That is, characterize the $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space of all $(a_{p,q}, b_I)$ such that $L(V) = L(V')$ whenever $V$ and $V'$ are smooth complex projective varieties related by the chosen equivalence, and identify those that are not invariant.

