A Finsler metric on a smooth manifold $M$ is a continuous function $F:TM\to[0,\infty)$ that is smooth and positive on $TM\setminus\{0\}$, positively homogeneous of degree one in the fiber variable (i.e. $F(x,\lambda v)=\lambda F(x,v)$ for all $\lambda>0$), and whose vertical Hessian $g_{ij}(x,v)=\tfrac12\partial^2 F^2/\partial v^i\partial v^j$ is positive definite on $TM\setminus\{0\}$. The flag curvature $K(x,v,P)$ generalizes the sectional curvature of Riemannian geometry; $F$ is said to have constant flag curvature $K\in\mathbb{R}$ if $K(x,v,P)\equiv K$ for all admissible flags $(x,v,P)$. A Finsler metric is called Zoll if every (oriented) geodesic of $F$ is closed. A Killing vector field of $F$ is a vector field on $M$ whose (local) flow consists of isometries of $F$.

Construct an explicit example of a Finsler metric $F$ on the 2-sphere $S^2$ such that:
(i) $F$ has constant flag curvature;
(ii) $F$ is not Zoll, i.e. at least one geodesic of $F$ is not closed;
(iii) $F$ admits no nontrivial Killing vector field.

Alternatively, prove that no such Finsler metric exists.

