Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth closed (compact, without boundary) connected pseudo-Riemannian manifold. Suppose that $L: TM \to TM$ is a smooth $(1,1)$-tensor field on $M$ satisfying all of the following conditions:

(i) $L \not\equiv 0$;
(ii) $L$ is self-adjoint with respect to $g$, i.e. $g(LX,Y) = g(X,LY)$ for every pair of vector fields $X,Y$ on $M$;
(iii) $L$ is parallel with respect to the Levi-Civita connection $\nabla$ of $g$, i.e. $\nabla L = 0$;
(iv) $L$ is nilpotent, i.e. there exists an integer $k \ge 1$ such that $L^{k} = 0$ as an endomorphism of $TM$.

Determine whether such a triple $(M,g,L)$ can exist. More precisely, decide whether there is any closed pseudo-Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$ admitting a non-zero, $g$-self-adjoint, $\nabla$-parallel, nilpotent $(1,1)$-tensor field, and if so, classify the possible $(M,g,L)$ up to local isometry.

