Consider LiHoF4, an insulating dipolar Ising ferromagnet in which the Ho3+ magnetic moments order below a Curie temperature T_c ≈ 1.53 K with the moments aligned along the crystalline c axis. Treat the system as a three-dimensional dipolar Ising ferromagnet on the LiHoF4 lattice. Theoretical analyses of such dipolar Ising ferromagnets predict that:

(a) the ground-state magnetic domain configuration consists of parallel, antiferromagnetically aligned sheets (alternating slabs of opposite magnetization along c);
(b) the characteristic width of these sheets grows as the demagnetization factor of the sample decreases, so that in the limit of an infinitely long, thin needle-shaped sample a state of uniform magnetization is realized;
(c) for sufficiently thick samples, the sheet domains develop a branched (tree-like) pattern near the surfaces, consistent with the Privman–Lifshitz / Landau-type branching scenario for uniaxial dipolar ferromagnets.

Problem. Determine, by direct experimental observation, the actual low-temperature (T < T_c) magnetic domain structure of pure (stoichiometric) LiHoF4 as a function of sample geometry and demagnetization factor, and decide whether the predicted phenomena (a)–(c) — parallel antiferromagnetically aligned sheet domains, monotonic dependence of sheet width on demagnetization factor with uniform magnetization in the needle limit, and branching of the domain pattern in thick samples — are quantitatively realized in this material.

