Let $G$ be a group. For a positive integer $n$, let $S_n$ denote the symmetric group on $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$, and define the normalized Hamming distance on $S_n$ by
$$d(\sigma,\tau) = \frac{1}{n}\,\#\{i\in\{1,\dots,n\} : \sigma(i)\neq\tau(i)\}.$$
Call $G$ sofic if for every finite subset $F\subseteq G$ with $1\in F$ and every $\varepsilon>0$ there exist a positive integer $n$ and a map $\varphi : F \to S_n$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\varphi(1)$ is the identity permutation;
\item $d(\varphi(gh),\varphi(g)\varphi(h)) < \varepsilon$ for all $g,h\in F$ with $gh\in F$;
\item for every $g\in F\setminus\{1\}$, the permutation $\varphi(g)$ has no fixed points (equivalently, $d(\varphi(g),\mathrm{id})=1$).
\end{enumerate}
Question: Is every group sofic?

