In the framework of semi-analytic modeling of galaxy formation — where galaxy properties (stellar mass, star-formation rate, cold gas content, etc.) are computed by applying parameterized physical recipes for gas cooling, star formation, stellar/AGN feedback, and galaxy merging on top of dark-matter halo merger trees extracted from a high-resolution cosmological N-body simulation — determine whether there exists a single such model, with physically plausible recipes and without resorting to unrestricted free tuning of every recipe (including the cooling prescription), that simultaneously reproduces all of the following observational constraints:

(i) the observed relation between specific star-formation rate (sSFR ≡ SFR/M_*) and stellar mass M_* of galaxies at redshift z = 0;
(ii) the galaxy stellar mass function (SMF) at z = 0 and its evolution with redshift;
(iii) galaxy two-point correlation functions (clustering as a function of stellar mass and/or luminosity);
(iv) cold-gas (atomic + molecular) mass fractions of galaxies as a function of stellar mass.

Either exhibit such a model and demonstrate the simultaneous fit, or show that within the standard SAM framework no such consistent parameter choice exists.

