In the standard three-flavour neutrino oscillation framework, the three known neutrino flavour eigenstates (electron, muon, tau) are unitary linear combinations of three mass eigenstates with masses m_1, m_2, m_3, related by the PMNS mixing matrix and characterized by two independent mass-squared splittings Δm²_21 ≈ 7.5×10⁻⁵ eV² and |Δm²_31| ≈ 2.5×10⁻³ eV², three mixing angles, and one CP-violating phase. Lorentz invariance and CPT invariance are assumed, so neutrinos and antineutrinos share the same mass spectrum and oscillation parameters, neutrinos travel at speeds indistinguishable from c at experimental precision, and matter effects are described by the Standard Model charged- and neutral-current interactions.

A number of experimental results are in tension with this minimal picture:
(a) the LSND excess of ν̄_e appearance in a ν̄_μ beam at L/E ~ 1 m/MeV;
(b) the MiniBooNE excess of ν̄_e- and ν_e-like events in ν̄_μ and ν_μ beams, including a low-energy excess;
(c) the reactor antineutrino anomaly: a deficit of ν̄_e at short baselines relative to recomputed reactor flux predictions;
(d) the gallium anomaly: a deficit of ν_e events in radiochemical source calibrations of GALLEX/SAGE-type detectors;
(e) reported asymmetries between neutrino and antineutrino oscillation parameters;
(f) possible seasonal/temporal variations in observed rates;
(g) a reported anomalous superluminal propagation velocity for muon neutrinos over a long baseline.

Question. Determine whether the anomalies (a)–(g) reflect genuine physics beyond the three-flavour oscillation framework. If so, construct a single quantitative theoretical extension—such as the addition of one or more sterile neutrino states mixing with the active flavours, CPT-violating mass spectra distinguishing ν from ν̄, or non-standard neutrino–matter interactions parametrized by Hermitian couplings ε_{αβ}—that simultaneously fits the magnitudes, energy and baseline dependences of all anomalies (a)–(g) while remaining consistent with: all null short- and long-baseline oscillation searches, atmospheric and solar neutrino data, cosmological bounds on extra relativistic species and on the sum of neutrino masses, and existing tests of Lorentz invariance and of the speed of neutrinos. Either exhibit such a model with explicit parameter values and predicted observables, or prove that no such single model exists within the stated constraints.

